GAL22V10
UA6Repl


AB1  AB2  AB3  AB4  AB5   AB6    STRB   CFG8M   CFG4M   CFG2M   BASEA  GND
RW   CFGIN NC1  ACPHASE  RST    BIN3   BIN2    BIN1    BIN0    NC2    DRVEN  VCC


; ============================================================
;   UA6 GAL22V10 - Draft 11 (do-nothing control experiment)
; ============================================================
;
;   VCD analysis (ua6_next.vcd power-on trace) showed that
;   Draft 10 causes UA4 to power up with /_GALCLK1 and
;   /_GALSTROBE ALREADY ASSERTED at #0. This propagates through
;   U14 (o19.oe = /i1) to assert /_OVR, which disables Amiga's
;   default DTACK path. With UA6 removed, UA4 powers up with
;   those outputs HIGH — clean boot.
;
;   The suspected cause: Draft 10's DRVEN equation can glitch
;   LOW during power-up because BASEA, /CFGIN, RW are all
;   undefined initially. Any brief coincident LOW state fires
;   DRVEN → UA4 pin 14 LOW → /_GALCLK1 self-latches asserted.
;
;   Draft 11 is a CONTROL EXPERIMENT: force every output Hi-Z
;   via tautological contradiction. UA6 becomes electrically
;   invisible except for its input capacitance. Should boot
;   identically to UA6-removed. If not → the chip's mere
;   presence (input capacitance loading) is the problem.
;
;   Once Draft 11 is confirmed as a clean baseline, Draft 12
;   will re-add DRVEN and ACPHASE with proper /_RST gating so
;   nothing drives until reset releases and inputs are stable.
;
; ============================================================


; --- ALL outputs forced Hi-Z (tautological contradiction on .E) ---

BIN0.T = VCC
BIN0.E = AB1 * /AB1

BIN1.T = VCC
BIN1.E = AB1 * /AB1

BIN2.T = VCC
BIN2.E = AB1 * /AB1

BIN3.T = VCC
BIN3.E = AB1 * /AB1

ACPHASE.T = VCC
ACPHASE.E = AB1 * /AB1

DRVEN.T = VCC
DRVEN.E = AB1 * /AB1

NC1.T = VCC
NC1.E = AB1 * /AB1

NC2.T = VCC
NC2.E = AB1 * /AB1


DESCRIPTION

UA6 GAL22V10 Draft 11 - do-nothing control experiment. Every
output forced Hi-Z. Should behave identically to UA6 removed.
Used to prove whether the chip's mere presence in the socket
is enough to break things, or whether it's specifically our
output drive that's the issue.
